  "type": "numerical",
  "level": 2,
  "question": {
    "text": "A spherical drop of liquid splits into <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1000</mn></math> identical spherical drops. If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></math> is the surface energy of the original drop and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></math> is the total surface energy of the resulting drops, the (ignoring evaporation),&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msub><mi mathvariant=\"italic\">u</mi><mi mathvariant=\"italic\">f</mi></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"italic\">u</mi><mi mathvariant=\"italic\">i</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfenced><mfrac><mn>10</mn><mi>x</mi></mfrac></mfenced></math>.&nbsp;Then value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi></math> is&nbsp;______:",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [],
  "correctValue": "1",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Let&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi></math>&nbsp;be the&nbsp;radius of bigger drop and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>r</mi></math>&nbsp;be the radius of smaller drop.</p><p>Here, volume will remain conserved, so&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></math>.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mi>&#960;</mi><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1000</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mi>&#960;</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mi>r</mi></math></p><p>As surface energy is stated as&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>S</mi><mi>A</mi></math>, here,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>S</mi></math>&nbsp;is surface tension and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi></math>&nbsp;is&nbsp;surface area.</p><p>Thus,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>S</mi><mfenced><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>&#960;</mi><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfenced></math>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>S</mi><mfenced><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>&#960;</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfenced><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>1000</mn></math></p><p>The ratio is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1000</mn><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>10</mn><mn>1</mn></mfrac></math></p><p>Hence, the value of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math>.</p>",
    "image": null
  },
